---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import EditorialLayout from "../../../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro";
import { wonderfulDigitalWorld as report } from "../../../data/wonderful-digital-world";
import { WORLD_VIEW_PATH } from "../../../data/world-view";
import { withBasePath } from "../../../utils/site-path";

const project = await getEntry("projects", "wonderful-digital-world");
if (!project) throw new Error("Missing Wonderful Digital World project entry");

const repository =
  "https://github.com/Wonderful-Digital-World/wonderful-digital-world";
const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const worldHref = withBasePath(WORLD_VIEW_PATH, basePath);

const openingSections = [
  {
    label: "Research question",
    id: "wdw-research-question",
    heading: "Why is the human still the orchestration layer?",
    paragraphs: [
      "That question is the reason Wonderful Digital World exists.",
      "A surprising amount of “using software” is really a person operating the control plane: remembering where information lives, moving it between tools, repeating context, noticing failed automation, checking whether something finished, and reconstructing decisions the computer has already seen.",
      "I do not want AI to replace the interesting human part. I want software to take responsibility for more of the software part.",
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Abstract",
    id: "wdw-abstract",
    heading:
      "The human provides the human things. The computer handles the computer things.",
    paragraphs: [
      "Wonderful Digital World is an experiment in a persistent computational environment: a world that continues to hold state, evidence, work, and outcomes even when a particular chat, model, interface, or process ends.",
      "Inside that world, deterministic software provides structure where guarantees matter—persistence, identity, provenance, permissions, routing, retries, lifecycle, and canonical state. AI is used where ambiguity earns its cost: interpretation, synthesis, prioritization, recommendations, and uncertain classification.",
      "The goal is not maximum autonomy. It is better placement of responsibility. Mechanical coordination should disappear into software; intent, judgment, consent, taste, and consequential decisions should remain meaningfully human.",
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Origin",
    id: "wdw-origin",
    heading: "A feature should reduce operation, not relocate it.",
    paragraphs: [
      "A chatbot can make a command easier to phrase while still leaving the human responsible for everything underneath it. If I still have to remember the context, tell the system which tool to use, notice when it failed, retry it, reconcile the result, and decide where the new state belongs, the interaction changed but the operating burden did not.",
      "WDW treats repeated coordination work as an architectural problem. Work becomes durable. Evidence keeps provenance. Domains retain ownership of their canonical state. Residents receive bounded context for their role and authority. Interfaces can change without becoming the world itself.",
      "That is also why the system can be whimsical on the surface without depending on the whimsy underneath. Telegram, a website, a tiny pixel world, voice, or something I have not tried yet can all be interfaces into the same persistent world.",
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "What it does",
    id: "wdw-what-it-does",
    heading: "Deterministic where possible; agentic where valuable.",
    paragraphs: [
      "LLMs are components, not the architecture. The architecture is the set of boundaries that let reasoning happen without confusing reasoning with authority: evidence, durable WorkItems, bounded residents, domain ownership, proposed actions, viewer-scoped projections, and explicit capability checks.",
      "The point is not to remove the human from the loop. It is to move the human to the part of the loop where being human is useful.",
    ],
  },
];
---

<EditorialLayout
  title={project.data.title}
  description={project.data.summary}
  eyebrow="Flagship report · Persistent environments"
>
  <header class="project-hero">
    <div>
      <h1>{project.data.title}</h1>
      <div class="deep-links">
        <a class="text-link" href={worldHref}>Enter World View</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="#evidence">See the evidence</a>
        <a class="text-link" href={repository} rel="noreferrer">
          Public repository ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="project-hero__meta" aria-label="Project status">
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>{project.data.status}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Public architecture and reference seam</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Evidence</dt><dd>
            Repository, contracts, tests, and fixture viewer
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </aside>
  </header>

  {
    openingSections.map((section) => (
      <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby={section.id}>
        <p class="section-label">{section.label}</p>
        <h2 id={section.id}>{section.heading}</h2>
        {section.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
          <p>{paragraph}</p>
        ))}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-world-view">
    <p class="section-label">D2 · Public projection</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-world-view">
      World View is a window, not the private runtime.
    </h2>
    <p>
      <a href={worldHref}>World View</a> renders the reviewed, checksummed public
      projection shipped with this site. It contains only explicitly published places,
      residents, activity, and attention. It does not connect to private resident
      memory, credentials, live runtime state, or unpublished data.
    </p>
    <div class="deep-links">
      <a href={worldHref}>Open World View</a>
      <a href={withBasePath("/projections/manifest.v1.json", basePath)}>
        Inspect the public manifest
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-loop">
    <p class="section-label">D1 · Operating loop</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-loop">Observe, reconcile, act, learn, continue.</h2>
    <p>
      The architecture treats continuity as a loop with inspectable transitions,
      rather than a conversation that starts over each time.
    </p>
    <ol class="operational-loop">
      {report.loop.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-objects">
    <p class="section-label">D2 · Architecture</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-objects">The objects that make the boundary legible.</h2>
    <dl class="object-ledger">
      {
        report.objects.map((object) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{object.term}</dt>
            <dd>{object.meaning}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="wdw-evidence">
    <p class="section-label">D2 · Evidence</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-evidence">What is public now—and what is not.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="evidence-table">
        <thead>
          <tr
            ><th>Part</th><th>Maturity</th><th>Public claim</th><th>Inspect</th
            ></tr
          >
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            report.evidence.map((item) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{item.part}</td>
                <td>{item.status}</td>
                <td>{item.detail}</td>
                <td>
                  <a href={item.inspect} rel="noreferrer">
                    Source ↗
                  </a>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-residents">
    <p class="section-label">D2 · Residents</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-residents">Named presences, bounded claims.</h2>
    <p>
      A resident may read, interpret, propose, abstain, and act only within an
      explicit capability. The public material does not expose private memory or
      claim a live society of autonomous characters.
    </p>
    <div class="resident-ledger">
      {
        report.residents.map((resident) => (
          <section>
            <p class="meta-line">{resident.status}</p>
            <h3>{resident.name}</h3>
            <p>{resident.note}</p>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-relations">
    <p class="section-label">D2 · Related systems</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-relations">Coordination without ownership.</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={withBasePath("/projects/the-human-model/", basePath)}
        >The Human Model</a
      > owns its domain evidence and evaluation. <a
        href={withBasePath("/projects/bridget/", basePath)}>Bridget</a
      > is an independent continuity system. Wonderful Digital World explores how
      a wider environment could coordinate bounded work around systems like these
      without becoming their compulsory interpreter.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-limits">
    <p class="section-label">D3 · Boundaries</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-limits">The public seam is intentionally narrow.</h2>
    <ul>{report.limitations.map((limitation) => <li>{limitation}</li>)}</ul>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="wdw-technical">
    <p class="section-label">D3 · Technical depth</p>
    <h2 id="wdw-technical">Inspect the contracts.</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>What the tested reference seam establishes</summary>
      <p>
        Public tests cover explicit authority, viewer-bounded projections,
        persistence before routing, idempotent transport, and final work
        outcomes. These are architecture guarantees, not evidence of a complete
        production environment.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What remains outside the public implementation</summary>
      <p>
        Production persistence, connectors, external tools, live projection,
        private lived data, credentials, and resident memory remain absent or
        intentionally excluded.
      </p>
    </details>
    <div class="deep-links">
      <a href={`${repository}/tree/main/docs`} rel="noreferrer"
        >Architecture docs ↗</a
      >
      <a href={`${repository}/tree/main/tests`} rel="noreferrer"
        >Public tests ↗</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
</EditorialLayout>
